[CONTEXT]
Multi-scale SDM: Applied to Picea abies (conifer) in Northern Europe with citizen science (iNaturalist, eBird). Key challenge: temporal mismatch between occurrence and environmental data. Climate: boreal, dispersal: wind.

[REASONING]
Species context: Picea abies in Northern Europe (boreal). Data: citizen science (iNaturalist, eBird). Step 1: Define a set of spatial grains (e.g., 1km, 5km, 10km, 50km) for each environmental predictor. Step 2: Compute focal statistics (mean, standard deviation) for each variable at each grain using moving windows. Step 3: Fit models with all scale combinations and select the optimal scale per variable using AIC. Step 4: Alternatively, include multiple scales of the same variable in a single model to capture cross-scale effects. Step 5: The scale of selection indicates the ecological neighborhood at which the species perceives each resource. Step 6: Compare model fit across species: generalists should respond to broader scales, specialists to finer scales.

[CODE]
```r
library(raster)

multiscale_sdm <- function(occ, env_stack, scales = c(1, 5, 10, 50)) {
  env_scaled <- list()
  for (s in scales) {
    if (s == 1) {
      env_scaled[[paste0('grain_', s, 'km')]] <- env_stack
    } else {
      w <- focalWeight(env_stack[[1]], s * 1000, type = 'circle')
      env_scaled[[paste0('grain_', s, 'km')]] <- stack(lapply(1:nlayers(env_stack),
        function(i) focal(env_stack[[i]], w, fun = mean, na.rm = TRUE)))
    }
  }
  all_vars <- do.call(stack, env_scaled)
  extracted <- raster::extract(all_vars, occ[, c('lon','lat')])
  models <- list()
  for (var in names(env_stack)) {
    scale_vars <- grep(paste0(var, '_grain'), names(all_vars), value = TRUE)
    formulas <- lapply(scale_vars, function(sv) as.formula(paste('presence ~', sv)))
    aics <- sapply(formulas, function(f) AIC(glm(f, data = occ, family = binomial)))
    best_scale <- scale_vars[which.min(aics)]
    models[[var]] <- list(best_scale = best_scale, aic = min(aics))
  }
  list(env_scaled = env_scaled, scale_selection = models)
}
```

