[CONTEXT]
Bayesian N-mixture with Stan: Applied to Octopus vulgaris (cephalopod) in Global temperate oceans with citizen science (iNaturalist, eBird). Key challenge: strong spatial autocorrelation among survey sites. Climate: temperate marine, dispersal: planktonic paralarvae.

[REASONING]
Species context: Octopus vulgaris in Global temperate oceans (temperate marine). Data: citizen science (iNaturalist, eBird). Step 1: Write the Stan model with latent abundance N_i ~ Poisson(lambda_i) and observation y_ij ~ Binomial(N_i, p_ij). Step 2: Marginalize over the discrete latent N using the log_mix function for computational efficiency. Step 3: Log(lambda_i) = gamma0 + gamma1 * elevation_i. Step 4: Logit(p_ij) = delta0 + delta1 * effort_ij. Step 5: Use weakly informative priors: gamma ~ normal(0, 2), delta ~ normal(0, 2). Step 6: The Stan implementation handles large K (maximum abundance) better than unmarked because it uses the log-scale marginalization.

[CODE]
```python
data {
  int<lower=1> S;
  int<lower=1> J;
  int<lower=0> y[S, J];
  vector[S] elevation;
  matrix[S, J] effort;
}
parameters {
  real gamma0;
  real gamma1;
  real delta0;
  real delta1;
}
model {
  vector[S] log_lambda = gamma0 + gamma1 * elevation;
  gamma0 ~ normal(0, 2);
  gamma1 ~ normal(0, 2);
  delta0 ~ normal(0, 2);
  delta1 ~ normal(0, 2);
  for (i in 1:S) {
    vector[301] lp;
    for (k in 0:300) {
      lp[k+1] = poisson_lpmf(k | exp(log_lambda[i])) +
        binomial_lpmf(y[i,] | k, inv_logit(delta0 + delta1 * effort[i,]));
    }
    target += log_sum_exp(lp);
  }
}
```

[CONTEXT]
Bayesian model averaging for SDM: Applied to Lynx lynx (Eurasian lynx) across Europe with presence-only data. Key challenge: model uncertainty — which variables to include, which algorithm to use. Climate: boreal-temperate, dispersal: terrestrial.

[REASONING]
Species context: Lynx lynx across Europe (boreal-temperate). Data: presence-only. Step 1: Define model space M: all combinations of 2^p possible variable subsets AND k candidate algorithms (MaxEnt, GLM, GAM, BRT, RF). Step 2: Fit all models and compute BIC or WAIC: BIC_m = -2 * logLik_m + k_m * log(n). Step 3: Posterior model probability: P(M_m|data) = exp(-0.5 * BIC_m) / sum_j exp(-0.5 * BIC_j). Step 4: Model-averaged prediction: E(presence|x) = sum_m P(M_m|data) * P(presence|x, M_m). Step 5: Variable importance: sum of posterior probabilities of all models containing variable v. Step 6: The BMA prediction typically outperforms any single model, especially with few occurrences. However, interpretation is more complex because the prediction is a weighted average across heterogeneous model structures.

[CODE]
```r
library(BMS)

bma_sdm <- function(occurrence, env_data, n_algorithms = 3) {
  models <- list()
  bics <- numeric(n_algorithms)
  for(k in 1:n_algorithms) {
    if(k == 1) models[[k]] <- glm(occurrence ~ ., data = env_data, family = binomial)
    if(k == 2) models[[k]] <- gam(occurrence ~ s(bio1) + s(bio12) + ., data = env_data, family = binomial)
    if(k == 3) models[[k]] <- gbm(occurrence ~ ., data = env_data, distribution = 'bernoulli', n.trees = 1000, interaction.depth = 3, shrinkage = 0.01)
    bics[k] <- BIC(models[[k]])
  }
  posterior_weights <- exp(-0.5 * (bics - min(bics))) / sum(exp(-0.5 * (bics - min(bics))))
  list(weights = posterior_weights, models = models, bics = bics)
}
```

