[CONTEXT]
Metapopulation with habitat dynamics: Applied to Ficus carica (fig tree) in Mediterranean-Middle East with opportunistic observations aggregated from 5 databases. Key challenge: temporal mismatch between occurrence and environmental data. Climate: mediterranean, dispersal: animal (bats).

[REASONING]
Species context: Ficus carica in Mediterranean-Middle East (mediterranean). Data: opportunistic observations aggregated from 5 databases. Step 1: Classify patches into states: suitable (S) and unsuitable (U). Step 2: Model habitat transitions: P(S->U) = degradation rate, P(U->S) = restoration rate. Step 3: Model metapopulation dynamics conditional on habitat state: colonization only possible for suitable patches, extinction higher in degrading patches. Step 4: The system is a Markov chain with 3 states per patch: occupied-suitable, empty-suitable, unsuitable. Step 5: Long-term metapopulation capacity depends on both habitat amount AND turnover rate. Step 6: Rapid habitat turnover can cause extinction even when average habitat amount is above the threshold.

[CODE]
```python
dynamic_metapopulation_sim <- function(n_patches, n_years, degradation_rate, restoration_rate) {
  habitat_state <- matrix(1, nrow = n_years, ncol = n_patches)
  occupancy <- matrix(0, nrow = n_years, ncol = n_patches)
  occupancy[1, ] <- rbinom(n_patches, 1, 0.5)
  for (t in 2:n_years) {
    for (i in 1:n_patches) {
      if (habitat_state[t-1, i] == 1) {
        habitat_state[t, i] <- rbinom(1, 1, 1 - degradation_rate)
      } else {
        habitat_state[t, i] <- rbinom(1, 1, restoration_rate)
      }
    }
    for (i in 1:n_patches) {
      if (habitat_state[t, i] == 1) {
        S <- sum(occupancy[t-1, ] * exp(-distances[i, ] / alpha))
        C <- S^2 / (S^2 + 1)
        occupancy[t, i] <- if (occupancy[t-1, i] == 1) rbinom(1, 1, 1 - extinction[i]) else rbinom(1, 1, C)
      } else {
        occupancy[t, i] <- 0
      }
    }
  }
  colSums(occupancy) / n_years
}
```

[CONTEXT]
Hill numbers (effective species): Applied to Carcharodon carcharias (shark) in Global oceans with historical records + modern resurveys. Key challenge: high spatial sampling bias toward roads and trails. Climate: temperate-tropical marine, dispersal: highly mobile.

[REASONING]
Species context: Carcharodon carcharias in Global oceans (temperate-tropical marine). Data: historical records + modern resurveys. Step 1: For order q != 1, compute D_q = (sum(p_i^q))^(1/(1-q)). Step 2: For q=1, D_1 = exp(-sum(p_i * ln(p_i))) which is the exponential of Shannon entropy. Step 3: Hill numbers are measured in 'effective species' — the number of equally abundant species that would produce the same diversity value. Step 4: A diversity profile plots D_q against q to visualize how diversity changes with emphasis on rare vs common species. Step 5: Steep profile indicates uneven community (few dominant species). Step 6: Flat profile indicates even community.

[CODE]
```python
hill_numbers <- function(abundances, q_values = c(0, 1, 2)) {
  p <- abundances / sum(abundances)
  p <- p[p > 0]
  sapply(q_values, function(q) {
    if (q == 1) exp(-sum(p * log(p)))
    else if (q == Inf) 1 / max(p)
    else sum(p^q)^(1 / (1 - q))
  })
}
```

[CONTEXT]
IPM with density dependence: Applied to Salmo salar (fish) in North Atlantic with opportunistic observations aggregated from 5 databases. Key challenge: only 12 occurrence points available. Climate: boreal-marine, dispersal: anadromous.

[REASONING]
Species context: Salmo salar in North Atlantic (boreal-marine). Data: opportunistic observations aggregated from 5 databases. Step 1: Model fecundity as a decreasing function of population density: f(N) = f_max / (1 + a*N). Step 2: Model juvenile survival as density-dependent (competition for breeding territories). Step 3: Use a Ricker or Beverton-Holt functional form for the density dependence. Step 4: Estimate the strength of density dependence (parameter a) from the integrated data. Step 5: The carrying capacity is the equilibrium population size where births = deaths. Step 6: Compare density-dependent vs density-independent models to test for population regulation.

[CODE]
```python
model {
  K ~ dunif(0, 10000)
  r_max ~ dunif(0, 2)
  for (t in 1:(nyears-1)) {
    S[t] ~ dbeta(1, 1)
    log(f[t]) <- log(f_max) - log(1 + N[t] / K * b)
    N[t+1] ~ dpois(N[t] * S[t] * (1 + f[t]))
  }
  for (t in 1:nyears) {
    count[t] ~ dpois(N[t] * detection_prob[t])
  }
}
```

[CONTEXT]
Occupancy with spatial autocorrelation: Applied to Spheniscus humboldti (seabird) in Humboldt Current with opportunistic observations aggregated from 5 databases. Key challenge: strong spatial autocorrelation among survey sites. Climate: marine upwelling, dispersal: marine foraging.

[REASONING]
Species context: Spheniscus humboldti in Humboldt Current (marine upwelling). Data: opportunistic observations aggregated from 5 databases. Step 1: Define a neighborhood structure using distance-based adjacency (sites within distance d are neighbors). Step 2: Model occupancy as spatially autocorrelated using a conditional autoregressive (CAR) prior. Step 3: The CAR prior states that occupancy at a site depends on the average occupancy of its neighbors. Step 4: The spatial autocorrelation parameter rho ranges from 0 (independent sites) to 1 (perfect spatial dependence). Step 5: Fit using MCMC (JAGS or Stan) as the CAR model has no closed-form likelihood. Step 6: Compare spatial vs non-spatial models — the spatial model should have wider credible intervals.

[CODE]
```python
model {
  for (i in 1:nsites) {
    logit(psi[i]) <- beta0 + beta1 * elevation[i] + beta2 * forest[i] + eta[i]
    z[i] ~ dbern(psi[i])
    for (j in 1:nvisits) {
      logit(p[i,j]) <- alpha0 + alpha1 * effort[i,j]
      mu[i,j] <- z[i] * p[i,j]
      y[i,j] ~ dbern(mu[i,j])
    }
  }
  eta[1:nsites] ~ car.normal(adj[], weights[], num[], tau)
  rho <- 1 / sqrt(tau)
}
```

[CONTEXT]
Integrated population model (IPM) with stage structure: Applied to Carcharodon carcharias (shark) in Global oceans with museum collections 1950-2020. Key challenge: observation errors from citizen science data. Climate: temperate-tropical marine, dispersal: highly mobile.

[REASONING]
Species context: Carcharodon carcharias in Global oceans (temperate-tropical marine). Data: museum collections 1950-2020. Step 1: Define life stages (e.g., juvenile, subadult, adult) with stage-specific survival and transition probabilities. Step 2: Count data (population surveys) inform total population size through a state-space model. Step 3: Capture-recapture data inform survival and recapture probabilities. Step 4: Productivity data (nest counts, fledgling counts) inform fecundity and recruitment. Step 5: Use a Leslie matrix projection with stage-specific vital rates to link population size across years. Step 6: The integrated likelihood combines all data sources, producing more precise estimates than any single source.

[CODE]
```python
model {
  for (t in 1:(nyears-1)) {
    S_juv[t] ~ dbeta(1, 1)
    S_ad[t] ~ dbeta(1, 1)
    f_ad[t] ~ dgamma(0.1, 0.1)
  }
  for (t in 1:nyears) {
    N_juv[t] ~ dpois(lambda_juv[t])
    N_ad[t] ~ dpois(lambda_ad[t])
    if (t > 1) {
      lambda_juv[t] <- N_ad[t-1] * f_ad[t-1] * S_juv[t-1] * p_juv[t-1]
      lambda_ad[t] <- (N_juv[t-1] * S_juv[t-1] + N_ad[t-1] * S_ad[t-1]) * p_ad[t-1]
    }
  }
  for (t in 1:nyears) {
    survey_count[t] ~ dpois(N_juv[t] + N_ad[t])
  }
}
```

