[CONTEXT]
Time-to-detection occupancy: Applied to Cryptobranchus alleganiensis (hellbender salamander) with snorkel surveys. Key challenge: detection probability depends on search effort — longer searches yield higher detection. Model time until first detection. Climate: temperate deciduous, dispersal: freshwater.

[REASONING]
Species context: Cryptobranchus alleganiensis in Appalachia (temperate deciduous). Data: snorkel surveys with search time. Step 1: Instead of binary detection (0/1) per visit, record time until detection: t_ij = minutes until first individual detected at site i, visit j. If not detected during T_max minutes, t_ij is right-censored at T_max. Step 2: Time-to-detection follows exponential distribution: f(t) = lambda_i * exp(-lambda_i * t), where lambda_i = r_i * p is the detection rate at occupied site i (r_i = abundance or availability, p = per-minute detection). Step 3: Occupancy component: z_i ~ Bernoulli(psi), and lambda_i = 0 if z_i = 0 (unoccupied). Likelihood for right-censored observations uses survival function S(T_max) = exp(-lambda * T_max). Step 4: Fit via Bayesian: estimate psi (occupancy), p_0 (baseline detection rate), and covariate effects on both. Step 5: Time-to-detection uses more information than binary detection data — a 1-minute detection and a 59-minute detection are both '1' in binary but very different in continuous. Step 6: Compare with standard occupancy — time-to-detection yields more precise occupancy estimates with fewer visits.

[CODE]
```r
library(nimble)

ttd_occupancy <- function(time_to_detection, T_max, env_covariates) {
  code <- nimbleCode({
    for(i in 1:N) {
      z[i] ~ dbern(psi[i])
      logit(psi[i]) <- beta0 + beta1 * covariate[i]
      lambda[i] <- z[i] * rate
    }
    for(i in 1:N) {
      for(j in 1:J) {
        is_censored[i,j] ~ dinterval(t[i,j], T_max[i,j])
        t[i,j] ~ dexp(lambda[i])
      }
    }
    psi ~ dbeta(1, 1)
    rate ~ dgamma(0.01, 0.01)
  })
  model <- nimbleModel(code, data = list(t = time_to_detection, T_max = T_max))
  mcmc <- nimbleMCMC(model, niter = 5000, nburnin = 2000)
  summary(mcmc)
}
```

[CONTEXT]
Metapopulation with habitat dynamics: Applied to Alces alces (ungulate) in Boreal North America with camera trap arrays (n=50 stations). Key challenge: only 12 occurrence points available. Climate: boreal, dispersal: long-distance terrestrial.

[REASONING]
Species context: Alces alces in Boreal North America (boreal). Data: camera trap arrays (n=50 stations). Step 1: Classify patches into states: suitable (S) and unsuitable (U). Step 2: Model habitat transitions: P(S->U) = degradation rate, P(U->S) = restoration rate. Step 3: Model metapopulation dynamics conditional on habitat state: colonization only possible for suitable patches, extinction higher in degrading patches. Step 4: The system is a Markov chain with 3 states per patch: occupied-suitable, empty-suitable, unsuitable. Step 5: Long-term metapopulation capacity depends on both habitat amount AND turnover rate. Step 6: Rapid habitat turnover can cause extinction even when average habitat amount is above the threshold.

[CODE]
```python
dynamic_metapopulation_sim <- function(n_patches, n_years, degradation_rate, restoration_rate) {
  habitat_state <- matrix(1, nrow = n_years, ncol = n_patches)
  occupancy <- matrix(0, nrow = n_years, ncol = n_patches)
  occupancy[1, ] <- rbinom(n_patches, 1, 0.5)
  for (t in 2:n_years) {
    for (i in 1:n_patches) {
