Metapopulation with habitat dynamics: Applied to Giraffa camelopardalis (mammal) in Sub-Saharan Africa with citizen science (iNaturalist, eBird). Key challenge: observation errors from citizen science data. Climate: savanna, dispersal: terrestrial.

[REASONING]
Species context: Giraffa camelopardalis in Sub-Saharan Africa (savanna). Data: citizen science (iNaturalist, eBird). Step 1: Classify patches into states: suitable (S) and unsuitable (U). Step 2: Model habitat transitions: P(S->U) = degradation rate, P(U->S) = restoration rate. Step 3: Model metapopulation dynamics conditional on habitat state: colonization only possible for suitable patches, extinction higher in degrading patches. Step 4: The system is a Markov chain with 3 states per patch: occupied-suitable, empty-suitable, unsuitable. Step 5: Long-term metapopulation capacity depends on both habitat amount AND turnover rate. Step 6: Rapid habitat turnover can cause extinction even when average habitat amount is above the threshold.

[CODE]
