[REASONING]
Species context: Thunnus albacares in Tropical oceans (tropical marine). Data: systematic field surveys (grid-based). Step 1: Classify patches into states: suitable (S) and unsuitable (U). Step 2: Model habitat transitions: P(S->U) = degradation rate, P(U->S) = restoration rate. Step 3: Model metapopulation dynamics conditional on habitat state: colonization only possible for suitable patches, extinction higher in degrading patches. Step 4: The system is a Markov chain with 3 states per patch: occupied-suitable, empty-suitable, unsuitable. Step 5: Long-term metapopulation capacity depends on both habitat amount AND turnover rate. Step 6: Rapid habitat turnover can cause extinction even when average habitat amount is above the threshold.

[CODE]
```python
dynamic_metapopulation_sim <- function(n_patches, n_years, degradation_rate, restoration_rate) {
  habitat_state <- matrix(1, nrow = n_years, ncol = n_patches)
  occupancy <- matrix(0, nrow = n_years, ncol = n_patches)
  occupancy[1, ] <- rbinom(n_patches, 1, 0.5)
  for (t in 2:n_years) {
    for (i in 1:n_patches) {
      if (habitat_state[t-1, i] == 1) {
        habitat_state[t, i] <- rbinom(1, 1, 1 - degradation_rate)
      } else {
        habitat_state[t, i] <- rbinom(1, 1, restoration_rate)
      }
    }
    for (i in 1:n_patches) {
      if (habitat_state[t, i] == 1) {
        S <- sum(occupancy[t-1, ] * exp(-distances[i, ] / alpha))
        C <- S^2 / (S^2 + 1)
        occupancy[t, i] <- if (occupancy[t-1, i] == 1) rbinom(1, 1, 1 - extinction[i]) else rbinom(1, 1, C)
      } else {
        occupancy[t, i] <- 0
      }
    }
  }
  colSums(occupancy) / n_years
}
```

