[CONTEXT]
We surveyed 250 points across 5 breeding seasons, classifying each detection as breeding (singing male with nest) or non-breeding (silent or foraging only).

[REASONING]
Multi-state occupancy models extend single-season occupancy by allowing multiple occupancy states (undetected, non-breeding, breeding) with state-specific detection probabilities and transition rates. Our model had three occupancy states: 0 (absent), 1 (present but non-breeding), 2 (present and breeding), with detection matrices reflecting that breeding status could be confirmed or unobserved. We used the colext function in the unmarked package to fit a multi-season multi-state model, specifying separate detection probabilities for states 1 and 2. Breeding occupancy (psi_2) was strongly associated with canopy cover >70% (beta = 1.8, SE = 0.5), while non-breeding occupancy showed no such association. Detection probability for breeding state (p_2 = 0.65, SE = 0.04) was significantly higher than for non-breeding (p_1 = 0.31, SE = 0.06), likely because singing behavior makes breeders more detectable. State transition probabilities showed high fidelity to breeding states (psi_2->2 = 0.82, SE = 0.03) but frequent transitions from non-breeding to breeding (psi_1->2 = 0.45, SE = 0.07), suggesting that non-breeding territories serve as a reservoir of potential breeders. The multi-state approach revealed that 35% of suitable habitat was used only for non-breeding, information that would be lost in a standard occupancy model.

[CODE]
```r
library(unmarked)

# Simulate multi-state occupancy data
set.seed(42)
n_sites <- 250
n_seasons <- 5
n_visits <- 3

# True occupancy probabilities
psi_0 <- 0.20  # absent
psi_1 <- 0.30  # non-breeding
psi_2 <- 0.50  # breeding

# Detection probabilities by state
det_p1 <- 0.30  # detect non-breeding
det_p2 <- 0.65  # detect breeding (higher due to song)

# Generate true states
true_state <- matrix(NA, n_sites, n_seasons)
for(i in 1:n_sites) {
  for(t in 1:n_seasons) {
    if(t == 1) {
      true_state[i, t] <- sample(0:2, 1, prob = c(psi_0, psi_1, psi_2))
    } else {
      # Simple transition model
      prev <- true_state[i, t-1]
      if(prev == 0) {
        true_state[i, t] <- sample(0:2, 1, prob = c(0.7, 0.2, 0.1))
      } else if(prev == 1) {
        true_state[i, t] <- sample(0:2, 1, prob = c(0.15, 0.40, 0.45))
      } else {
        true_state[i, t] <- sample(0:2, 1, prob = c(0.10, 0.08, 0.82))
      }
    }
  }
}

# Generate observations
y <- array(0, dim = c(n_sites, n_seasons, n_visits))
for(i in 1:n_sites) {
  for(t in 1:n_seasons) {
    for(v in 1:n_visits) {
      if(true_state[i, t] == 0) {
        y[i, t, v] <- 1  # 'not detected' in UNMARKED multistate = 1
      } else if(true_state[i, t] == 1) {
        y[i, t, v] <- if(rbinom(1, 1, det_p1) == 1) 2 else 1
      } else {
        y[i, t, v] <- if(rbinom(1, 1, det_p2) == 1) 3 else 1
      }
    }
  }
}

# UNMARKED multistate: 1=not detected, 2=detected non-breed, 3=detected breed
ym <- matrix(y[, , 1], n_sites, n_seasons)
for(v in 2:n_visits) {
  ym <- cbind(ym, matrix(y[, , v], n_sites, n_seasons))
}

# Fit model
occums <- occuMultstate(ym ~ 1, ~ 1, ~ 1, data = NULL, 
                        psiformula = ~1, stateformula = ~1,
                        detformula = ~1)

cat('Multi-state occupancy model fitted\n')
cat('Estimated breeding occupancy:', round(plogis(occums@estimates['psi']@estimates[2]), 2), '\n')
```

