Species context: Salmo salar in North Atlantic (boreal-marine). Data: systematic field surveys (grid-based). Step 1: Structure the ecological time series as a state-space model: observed y_t depends on latent state z_t with observation error. Step 2: Latent state evolves according to a process model: z_t = f(z_{t-1}, covariates, parameters) + process error. Step 3: Specify priors for all parameters: process variance, observation variance, covariate effects. Step 4: Fit in Stan using Hamiltonian Monte Carlo (HMC) — more efficient than Gibbs for correlated parameters. Step 5: Forecast: sample from the posterior predictive distribution for future time steps. Step 6: Compute forecast skill metrics: CRPS (continuous ranked probability score), coverage of prediction intervals.

[CODE]
```python
data {
  int<lower=0> T;
  int<lower=0> y[T];
  vector[T] temperature;
}
parameters {
  real<lower=0> r;
  real<lower=0> K;
  real beta;
  real<lower=0> sigma_proc;
  real<lower=0> sigma_obs;
  vector[T] logN;
}
model {
  r ~ normal(0, 1);
  K ~ lognormal(5, 1);
  beta ~ normal(0, 0.5);
  sigma_proc ~ exponential(1);
  sigma_obs ~ exponential(1);
  logN[1] ~ normal(5, 1);
  for (t in 2:T) {
    logN[t] ~ normal(logN[t-1] + r * (1 - exp(logN[t-1]) / K) + beta * temperature[t], sigma_proc);
  }
  for (t in 1:T) {
    y[t] ~ poisson_log(logN[t]);
  }
}
```

